## Decoding the question
**"Critically examine and evaluate"** asks you to assess **achievements and limits** fairly, not to write a eulogy. **"Achievements and activism"** covers both what she has won and what she does. **"Local and global contexts"** means two clear sections: her impact **in Pakistan** and her impact **worldwide**. End with a balanced judgement.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Who she is
**Malala Yousafzai**, born in **Swat** in ==1997==, survived a Taliban assassination attempt in ==2012== and became the world's best-known advocate of girls' education and, in ==2014==, the youngest **Nobel Peace Prize** laureate.

#### ii) The argument
Malala's greatest achievement is **global**: she turned girls' education into a cause that world leaders could not ignore and built an organisation that funds local activists in many countries. Her **local** impact in Pakistan is real but modest beside the scale of the country's education crisis, and she remains a divisive figure at home. Her story shows both the power of individual courage and the limits of what any individual can do without **state action**.

### 2. Background
Malala was born on ==12 July 1997== in **Mingora**. Her father, **Ziauddin Yousafzai**, a teacher and education activist who ran the **Khushal Public School**, encouraged her to speak out. From ==2007== the **Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan** under **Maulana Fazlullah** took control of much of Swat, banning music and women's public life, and in ==December 2008== ordered all **girls' schools** closed; more than 100 were destroyed. A military operation in ==2009== drove the Taliban out.

### 3. Early activism
In ==January 2009==, aged **11**, Malala began writing a diary for **BBC Urdu** under the pen name "**Gul Makai**", describing life under the Taliban and her fear of losing her education. She appeared in a *New York Times* documentary and spoke on Pakistani television. In ==2011== she received Pakistan's first **National Youth Peace Prize**. Even before the attack, she had become a local voice for girls' right to learn.

### 4. The attack of 9 October 2012
On ==9 October 2012== a Taliban gunman boarded her school bus, asked "Who is Malala?", and shot her in the head; two classmates, **Shazia Ramzan** and **Kainat Riaz**, were also wounded. She was flown to **Birmingham** in the United Kingdom and recovered. The attack made her a global symbol: an attempt to silence a girl for wanting to go to school instead amplified her voice worldwide.

### 5. Global achievements 1: the UN speech
On her 16th birthday, ==12 July 2013==, declared "**Malala Day**" by the UN, she addressed the **UN Youth Assembly**: "**One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world.**" The speech made girls' education a mainstream international cause and was followed by global campaigns for free, compulsory schooling.

### 6. Global achievements 2: the Fund, books and the Nobel Prize

#### i) Malala Fund
In ==2013==, with her father, she co-founded the **Malala Fund**, which invests in local "**Education Champions**" and advocates for policies so that girls complete 12 years of free, safe, quality education — in **Pakistan, Afghanistan, Nigeria, India, Brazil, Ethiopia** and among refugees in **Lebanon**.

#### ii) Books
***I Am Malala*** (==2013==) became a global best-seller; *We Are Displaced* (==2019==) told the stories of refugee girls.

#### iii) Awards
The European Parliament's **Sakharov Prize** (==2013==) and the **Nobel Peace Prize** (==10 December 2014==), shared with **Kailash Satyarthi**, made her the youngest laureate at **17**. She later became a **UN Messenger of Peace** (==2017==) and graduated from **Oxford** (==2020==).

::: fig timeline | Malala's activism, local and global.
@layout vertical
2009: BBC Urdu diary as Gul Makai (local)
2011: National Youth Peace Prize (local)
2012: shot by the Taliban (9 October)
2013: UN speech; Malala Fund; I Am Malala (global)
2014: Nobel Peace Prize, aged 17 (global)
2017: UN Messenger of Peace (global)
2018: first return visit to Pakistan (local)
2025: Islamabad conference on girls' education (local and global)
:::

### 7. Global context
Her activism helped keep **girls' education** at the centre of the **Sustainable Development Goals** (SDG 4, ==2015==). Since the Taliban's return to power in Afghanistan in ==2021== and their ban on girls' secondary and higher education, she has been a leading voice calling for "**gender apartheid**" to be recognised as a crime in international law. She has also championed refugee girls displaced by conflict in Syria and elsewhere. Her symbolic power gives such causes a hearing in capitals and international forums.

### 8. Local achievements
- **Shangla school:** she used her Nobel prize money to support a school for girls in her ancestral district of **Shangla**.
- **Grants:** by ==2025== the Malala Fund reported investing about **14.6 million dollars** in Pakistan since 2013, supporting local organisations that work to get girls into secondary school, and providing emergency grants after the ==2022== floods.
- **Advocacy:** the Fund has pressed federal and provincial governments to spend more on education and to enforce **Article 25A** (free and compulsory education to age 16).
- **Inspiration:** she has shown millions of Pakistani girls that a girl from a conservative region can become a global leader.

### 9. Local context
In ==January 2025== she returned to Islamabad to speak at the **Muslim World League**'s conference on **Girls' Education in Muslim Communities**, which issued an **Islamabad Declaration** affirming girls' education as a religious obligation and a social necessity. Yet the local context remains grim: Pakistan has more than **25 million** out-of-school children, most of them girls; female literacy is about ==53%==; and girls' secondary schools are scarce in rural areas. Against this scale, any single organisation's impact is necessarily limited.

### 10. Criticism
1. **"Western agent":** some Pakistanis accuse her of serving a Western agenda and shaming Pakistan abroad.
2. **Conspiracy theories:** some even claim the attack was staged, despite overwhelming evidence.
3. **"I am not Malala":** in ==2014== the **All Pakistan Private Schools Federation** observed an "I am not Malala" day and banned her book, objecting to passages on religion.
4. **Selective attention:** critics ask why the world honoured her while ignoring other victims, such as civilians killed in drone strikes.
5. **Distance:** for security reasons she lives abroad, and some argue her influence on the ground is limited.

### 11. Evaluation

#### i) What she achieved
Global agenda-setting; an organisation investing in local activists; a moral voice on Afghanistan; and a powerful symbol that has inspired girls worldwide.

#### ii) Honest limits
Pakistan's education crisis is driven by low spending, insecurity, poverty, early marriage and a shortage of girls' schools — problems that require **state action**, not an individual's campaign. Her polarising image at home may reduce her influence with some communities.

#### iii) A fair judgement
Many of the criticisms reflect **politics and conspiracy** more than evidence; the limits reflect the **scale** of the problem, not a failure of her activism.

### 12. Conclusion
Malala Yousafzai's achievements are extraordinary: from a schoolgirl's diary in Taliban-held Swat to the Nobel Peace Prize and a global foundation, she made girls' education a cause the world could not ignore. Her **global** impact is profound; her **local** impact is genuine but modest beside Pakistan's 25 million out-of-school children. The best tribute to her activism would be for the Pakistani state to do what no individual can — build and protect schools for every girl.

## Why this answer scores
- Organises achievements into **local** and **global** contexts, exactly as asked.
- Is genuinely **critical**: it weighs criticism and limits as well as achievements.
- Uses accurate **dates and facts** (1997, 2009, 2012, 2013, 2014, 2025; $14.6m; 25 million).
- A **timeline figure** separates local and global milestones.
